1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
From video to robot learning. Rohith Iruku, University of Guelph, with the V2D Track 3 team.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:13,000
The workflow preserves a chain from source observations to reconstructed scenes and simulated outputs.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Keep calibrated camera geometry, frame timing and validity flags attached to the original video. The scored roster contains 20 episodes and 5,181 frames.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Object masks, stereo depth and 6D tracking are inspected in image space. The footage shown is a released-CAD development diagnostic; the scored path uses reconstructed own-geometry.

5
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Development examples reveal how occlusion, different shapes and hand contact affect geometric tracking.

6
00:00:49,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Hand recovery uses temporal fitting and fixed cameras. Observed hand evidence and inferred gaps remain distinguishable during review.

7
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Inspect the scene in 3D. Camera conventions, mesh identities and observed-frame flags are checked before retargeting.

8
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Retargeted Dex3 commands are tested against simulation contacts and dynamics. These diagnostic replay views are not learned-policy evaluations; the physical task was not accepted.

9
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:47,000
The submitted PPO baseline trained 20 per-episode policies for 100 iterations each. A separate fresh FlashSAC pilot on episode 001 completed five million environment steps and 153,122 updates. That pilot was not promoted and did not establish physical task success.

10
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:03,000
The recorded public PPO baseline scored ADD-AUC 0.17749 and object Chamfer distance 0.53479 cm. Both success-rate metrics were zero. RPE was 3465.19732 cm and MPPE was 5125.70634 cm. Geometry coverage and physical execution remain separate outcomes.

11
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:12,000
An inspectable workflow from reconstruction through policy training and evaluation. Rohith Iruku and the V2D Track 3 team. Built on the credited open robotics and reconstruction projects.
